In article <1992Sep11.040932.24578@tamsun.tamu.edu> wrr3118@tamsun.tamu.edu (Rick Russell) writes:
>A long time ago, I posted a question about fixing Omega's restore-game
>bug for compilation with SunOS 4.1.1. A kind colleague found the
>troublesome code, and provided a simple patch.
>The problem is in the source file 'osave.c'. Open the file and
>cruise down to line 607. You will find the following lines: 
>See where it says 'sizeof(int)' above? That's the problem. Change it
>to read:
>     for (i=0;i<TOTALITEMS;i++) 
>       fread((char *)&(Objects[i].known),sizeof(Objects[i].known),1,fd);
>     calc_melee();
>Ta da! I compiled with gcc 2.0 (-traditional) and it works just fine!

For some reason, every so often it is asserted that there is such a bug and
tries to fix it... The fact is, the last official release of Omega, made in
mid-1989, did *not* have this bug.

I *just* checked ftp.uu.net, which is one of the official archive sites for
comp.sources.games, and though the files haven't changed in over 3 years,
there is no such bug. In fact, the correct code in the archive is identical to
the "fixed" version provided above. This version of Omega (0.75, called Omega3
in the archives) was never released with this bug.

There was a *pre-release* version of the game which did have this bug, but it
was fixed before ever being posted to comp.sources.games or being archived.
Probably there are some places which obtained the pre-release sources and have
set them up for ftp as being the actual sources, but in fact they are not the
sources for the released version of the game.

Please only use an official archive site (such as ftp.uu.net). You'll save
yourselves a lot of frustration and time tracking down nonexistent bugs.
